{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fisher exact test fisher exact test","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md","fileName":"Fisher Exact Test.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fisher Exact Test","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 18:44\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Descriptive Health Statistics]] · [[Statistical Inference and Resampling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Descriptive Health Statistics","Statistical Inference and Resampling","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fisher exact test fisher exact test","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fisher Exact Test.md","fileName":"Fisher Exact Test.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fisher Exact Test","heading":"Fisher Exact Test","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Fisher's exact test evaluates association in a contingency table by calculating probabilities under fixed marginal totals. It does not rely on the large-sample chi-squared approximation and is therefore useful when cells are small.\n\nThe test provides evidence about dependence between the categorical variables, not the size or practical importance of the association. Frequencies and proportions should still accompany its p-value.\n\nThe source motivates the method through Fisher's tea-tasting experiment: under random guessing and fixed margins, the probability of each table follows from the possible label arrangements. This makes the test an exact association procedure rather than a normal or t approximation.","tags":["baby"],"links":[